# Wild Rover - Iers/Engels volkslied
# Traditioneel volkslied (Roud 1173), mogelijk teruggaand op een 17e-eeuwse Engelse
# broadside; gedrukte versies uit 1813-1845 (Wikipedia (en), "The Wild Rover"). Publiek domein.
# Overgenomen uit WILD_RO.VER (WordPerfect 5.1, 1991) en Wild rover.doc (Word 97-2003, 2005); beide
# bestanden hebben dezelfde tekst en akkoorden. De akkoordregels zijn langer dan de tekst
# (proportioneel lettertype) en zijn naar de regellengte geschaald.
# De bron noemt het refrein na het eerste couplet; het is hier na elk couplet herhaald.
{title: Wild Rover}
{meta: id wild-rover}
{meta: language en}
{lyricist: traditioneel}
{composer: traditioneel}
{copyright: Public domain}
{meta: license public-domain}
{meta: source Overgenomen uit WILD_RO.VER (WordPerfect 5.1, 1991) en Wild rover.doc (2005); herkomst via Wikipedia (en)}
{meta: tag kampvuur}
{meta: tag volkslied}
{key: G}

{start_of_verse}
I've [G]been a wild ro[D]ver [G]for many a [D]year
[G]and I've spent all [D]my money on [G]whiskey [D]and [G]beer
[G]But now I'm retur[D]ning [G]with gold in great [D]store
[G]and I never will play the wild ro[G]ver no [D]more[G]
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_chorus}
[D]And it's no, [D7]nay, never
[G]No, nay, never no [D]more
I [G]will play the wild ro[C]ver
no ne[G]ver .............. no [D]more[G]
{end_of_chorus}

{start_of_verse}
I went to an alehouse I used to frequent
I told the landlady my money was spend
I asked her for credit, she answered me nay
such a custom as yours I can have every day
{end_of_verse}

{chorus}

{start_of_verse}
I brought up from me pocket ten sovereigns bright
and landlady's eyes opened wide with delight
She said: "I have whiskey's and wines of the best"
and the words that she told me were only in jest
{end_of_verse}

{chorus}

{start_of_verse}
I'll go home to my parents, confess what I've done
and I'll ask them to pardon their prodigal son
And when they've caressed me as oft times before
I never will play the wild rover no more
{end_of_verse}

{chorus}
